<BlogPost title="Why Dealers Are Turning to Custom Paint with Sherwin-Williams Colors" description="Eclipse and Shiloh's Custom Paint Program lets dealers spec any Standard Sherwin-Williams color on cabinetry. Here's how it works." author="Ben Miller" publishedDate="2025-07-18" modifiedDate="2026-10-29">

<blockquote><strong>Update (2026):</strong> For the current step-by-step ordering process — sample sizes and prices, the Eclipse edge-band check, the Custom Finish Approval Form, and 2020 Design Flex setup — see <a href="/playbook/custom-paint-color-drenching/">the Custom Paint ordering guide</a>.</blockquote>

<p>Looking to win over homeowners with a bold or custom finish? WW Wood Products' <a href="/brands/eclipse-cabinetry/">Eclipse</a> and <a href="/brands/shiloh-cabinetry/">Shiloh</a> cabinet lines offer a Custom Paint Program — unlocking limitless design potential by allowing dealers to spec any "Standard" paint color from Sherwin-Williams.</p>

<blockquote>"We've seen increasing demand from designers who want to match cabinet finishes with other elements in the home — this program gives them the tools."</blockquote>

<h2>How It Works</h2>
<ul>
<li>Pick a Sherwin-Williams "Standard" color (e.g., SW6671)</li>
<li>Order a custom-color sample (CM 8½ × 11 at $350 list, or CM 12½ × 15½ at $585 list) and submit the Custom Finish Approval Form with your order</li>
<li>Custom Paint adds 20%, on top of your species upcharge (e.g. 20% + 8% Maple = 28%)</li>
<li>Orders under $15,000 list add a $750 list custom paint fee — there is no order minimum</li>
<li>No added lead time if the sample door is received before the project order is submitted</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why It Matters for Your Showroom</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Design Flexibility</strong> = fewer lost jobs to local shops offering full custom</li>
<li><strong>Familiar Color System</strong> = confidence for both designers and end-users</li>
<li><strong>Streamlined Process</strong> = samples approved before build</li>
</ul>

</BlogPost>
